WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.979
A reliable receipt must mean something precise.

00:00:02.979 --> 00:00:07.352
In this case, it means the file and authoritative
record are durable.

00:00:07.352 --> 00:00:11.978
Notification can follow later through preserved
pending work and retries.

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:16.731
Stable request identity lets a repeated request
return the recorded result.

00:00:16.731 --> 00:00:22.878
Check partial failures and concurrent duplicates
instead of assuming all writes succeed together.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.176
Use the same retry scenario to connect four views.

00:00:26.176 --> 00:00:32.662
Domain relationships explain the submission, runtime
processes explain cooperation, modules explain

00:00:32.662 --> 00:00:36.855
code ownership and deployment explains where state
is reachable.

00:00:36.855 --> 00:00:43.342
The views should agree on one outcome: a valid retry
can find the saved result even when it reaches

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:45.504
a different application instance.

00:00:45.504 --> 00:00:49.856
Review architecture by comparing intended rules with
actual behavior.

00:00:49.856 --> 00:00:55.849
An early confirmation violates the durability
promise and is erosion; an undocumented compliant

00:00:55.849 --> 00:00:56.795
route is drift.

00:00:56.795 --> 00:01:00.454
Recover structure using the evidence your role can
access.

00:01:00.454 --> 00:01:06.383
Label claims as observed, inferred or unknown so the
review remains honest about what has been

00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:07.140
established.
